+++ b/docs/implementation.md
@@ -0,0 +1,90 @@
+# Quizzy implementation notes
+
+- Rails 8.1, SQLite. Production data is in `storage/production.sqlite3` (including SQLite WAL/SHM sidecars). No external services are used.
+- `bin/serve` prepares/migrates the database, compiles the local assets, and execs Puma on `0.0.0.0:$PORT` (default 3000). It forces production mode. Set `SECRET_KEY_BASE`, `QUIZZY_MODERATOR_USERNAME`, and `QUIZZY_MODERATOR_PASSWORD` before starting it. HTTPS redirects are disabled and all hosts are accepted.
+- All moderator controllers inherit HTTP Basic authentication. Missing environment credentials fail closed; they are never given fallback values in the application.
+- Each question has ordered JSON option objects (`text`, `correct`), an explicit position, and an active flag. Keeping options with the question makes validation and saving atomic. Raw submitted rows are retained separately while rendering validation failures; blank option text is omitted only from the persisted options.
+- Reordering normalizes positions inside a quiz transaction. Gaps after deleting questions are harmless; display order and active-question numbering are computed independently.
+- The supplied stylesheet and Inter font are served locally. Small responsive/accessibility additions are in `application.css`. Lists use real list items; switches use real checkboxes.
+- Plain Rails forms use CSRF protection. A small local JavaScript file implements additional option rows, immediate checkbox submission, and native delete confirmations. No Turbo or JavaScript build tooling is needed.
+- Preview receives a snapshot of active questions in order. Answers and score live only in JavaScript memory: no cookies, local storage, session storage, database writes, or requests record preview answers. Reloading or “Start over” resets them. Correct flags are deliberately exposed because this is an authenticated moderator preview, not a competitive player endpoint.
+
+## Verification
+
+```sh
+bundle check
+bin/rails test
+```
+
+With a running server, run the Chromium smoke test with the matching credentials:
+
+```sh
+QUIZZY_MODERATOR_USERNAME=moderator QUIZZY_MODERATOR_PASSWORD=your-password \
+  bundle exec ruby script/browser_smoke.rb
+```
+
+`APP_URL` defaults to `http://localhost:3000`. The script opens the design HTML exports in Chromium, exercises the app at desktop and phone widths, and writes screenshots under `storage/`. It creates and deletes its own quiz. Ferrum drives the installed Chromium directly, without downloading ChromeDriver.
+
+## Portable quiz JSON
+
+- The quiz page's **Export** link renders a single, HTML-escaped `<pre>` containing pretty-printed JSON. `QuizTransfer.export` explicitly selects only `title`, ordered `questions`, and each question's `text`, `active`, and ordered `options` (`text`, `correct`). Inactive questions are included. No IDs, timestamps, or preview state are exported.
+- **Import quiz** on `/moderate` accepts pasted **Quiz JSON**. GET and POST `/moderate/quizzes/import`, and the member export route, inherit moderator authentication. Successful imports redirect to a newly created quiz, including when a title already exists.
+- `QuizTransfer.import` requires the documented object/array/string types and actual JSON booleans. Missing `active` defaults to true and missing `correct` to false; explicit nulls and string/number booleans are invalid. Extra keys are ignored, never mass-assigned.
+- The importer uses the same `Question#option_rows` normalization and model validations as the question form: blank rows are ignored, at least two nonblank options and at least one correct answer are required. Multiple correct answers and empty quizzes are supported.
+- All writes share one database transaction. Any malformed structure or invalid question rolls back the quiz and all its questions. Error pages retain the pasted input and identify the failing question where applicable.
+- Regression coverage: `bin/rails test test/integration/quiz_transfer_test.rb`. Production browser check: `bundle exec ruby script/transfer_smoke.rb` with matching `QUIZZY_MODERATOR_*` credentials and optional `APP_URL`. It creates and deletes its own test quizzes.
+
+## Live games
+
+- **Start a game** creates a new `Game`, snapshots the quiz title and ordered active questions, and opens `/moderate/games/:id`. Each game has a unique random join code, its own players, and its own answers. Later quiz edits cannot change an existing game. The quiz page lists past games, so moderators can reopen a screen after losing a tab. Deleting a quiz also deletes its games, players, and answers.
+- The state machine is `waiting → open → closed → open … → closed → ended`. Empty quizzes transition directly from waiting to ended. Commands include the expected phase and question index; replayed/double-clicked or stale commands cannot advance a different question. Joins, answers, and transitions use the same game transaction/lock. Rails 8.1’s SQLite adapter uses immediate transactions, serializing finish/answer races across connections and Puma processes. Unique database indexes enforce one identity per game/browser and one answer per player/question.
+- Joining is public at `/play/:code`. An HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, signed random cookie remembers the browser for one year. Only its SHA-256 digest is stored with each game membership; no identity or player ID supplied in an answer request is trusted. Membership is per game, and duplicate joins return the existing player. Moderator game endpoints inherit the existing environment-configured HTTP Basic authentication. All mutations retain Rails CSRF protection.
+- `GameScreen` is the allow-listed presentation boundary. Public nonmembers receive only the invitation (or game-over screen). Joined players receive question text and option text, never correctness flags or distributions before closure. Correctness is calculated server-side. The game’s raw JSON snapshot is never serialized to a public endpoint. Changes replace the single answer record atomically; results count each player’s last choice.
+- All live updates use same-origin HTTP, not external services, sockets, or a job worker. The initial page is server-rendered. The client polls about once per second (three seconds in background tabs), sending an explicit ETag. Unchanged screens return `304` without markup. A change returns only the rendered screen fragment; no browser navigation occurs. A single request is in flight per screen, actions cancel stale polls, and responses are sequenced to avoid an old response replacing a newer state. Polling has jitter, an eight-second timeout covering the full body, capped exponential retry, and immediate resync when the browser comes online or is foregrounded. State is persisted in SQLite, so application restarts and dropped connections do not lose progress.
+- Presence is a **45-second lease** refreshed by player polls, with a write at most once per ten seconds per player. A dropped connection does not instantly remove a player on unreliable Wi-Fi. After the lease expires, the lobby/current-player denominator excludes them. The live answer numerator counts answers from those currently present players, so the moderator can see how many connected participants are still thinking. Closed option totals retain **all** submitted votes, even from disconnected players. Reconnecting renews presence and restores the same identity and answer. Background tabs continue heartbeating; no unreliable unload request is required.
+- QR codes are generated locally by `rqrcode` as SVG, including a quiet zone, from exactly the absolute URL displayed alongside them. The SVG contains only generated geometric paths. The QR gems are pinned and installed; neither fonts, scripts, QR generation nor game updates require external network access.
+
+### Live verification
+
+```sh
+bundle check
+bin/rails test
+# With bin/serve running using the matching moderator credentials:
+bundle exec ruby script/live_smoke.rb
+```
+
+The browser test uses four isolated Chromium browsers, exercises the full game (including multiple correct options, changed picks, inactive questions, late joins, remembered identity, every feedback state, game-over restrictions, independent games, and Wi-Fi loss), checks that live updates never navigate, and saves screenshots under `storage/`. `APP_URL` and `QUIZZY_MODERATOR_*` work as in the other smoke scripts. Integration tests check public payload privacy, ETags, invalid input, expired/reconnected presence, stale transitions, and identity isolation; committed-transaction model tests exercise simultaneous joins, starts, answers, and finish commands.
+
+## Prize scoring and near-instant updates
+
+- Quiz settings include **Number of winners**, an integer >= 1, default 3. Games snapshot this value alongside the questions. JSON export includes `number_of_winners`; older imports without it default to 3. Invalid imported settings roll back the import.
+- Opening a question records one server timestamp, `question_opened_at`. Each accepted pick overwrites the answer's integer `elapsed_microseconds`, including a repeated pick of the same option. The shared game lock serializes opens, picks, closes, and winner selections. Client clocks and submitted timing values are never trusted.
+- Each final correct choice earns one point. Leaderboard order is descending points, then ascending sum of correct-answer durations, then player ID for exactly equal totals. Wrong or missing answers contribute neither points nor time. All registered players remain ranked, even if disconnected. Points are rendered only after the game ends.
+- `Top N` takes the first N positive-scoring players. `Draw` guarantees prizes above the N-th player's point total and samples without replacement from the entire tied point group for remaining prizes, ignoring speed. Every click replaces the selection; a redraw may legitimately return the same winners. If fewer than N players have points, all of them win. Reset clears every mark. Winner flags persist in SQLite and do not alter leaderboard ordering.
+- **The old polling-only transport described above is superseded.** Rails Action Cable publishes post-commit invalidations for game transitions, answers, joins, and winner changes. Solid Cable shares notifications across Puma processes using the primary SQLite database in `storage/`, polled internally every 50 ms. No Redis, external service, worker, or CDN is needed. Its message table is created by the normal app migration, and old messages are trimmed by Solid Cable.
+- The public game-code channel carries only `{changed: true}`. It cannot accept moderator actions or reveal question keys, scores, names, or player credentials. Browsers immediately fetch their existing identity-scoped screen endpoint, preserving Basic authentication and CSRF protection for moderator writes. The official Action Cable client is served locally from the installed Rails gem. Same-origin WebSocket connections work on whichever HTTP host serves the app.
+- Recovery polling/ETags, reconnect resynchronization, and heartbeats remain. Push notifications coalesce during requests and writes, then trigger a fresh fetch, so a notification cannot be lost behind an in-flight request. Phones and additional big-screen tabs receive question transitions and winner changes without navigation.
+- Historical games created before the timing migration have zero durations for already-stored answers because no original opening time was recorded. New questions and games record timing normally.
+
+### Prize verification
+
+```sh
+bundle check
+bin/rails test
+# Against bin/serve with matching QUIZZY_MODERATOR_* credentials:
+bundle exec ruby script/prizes_smoke.rb
+bundle exec ruby script/live_smoke.rb
+```
+
+`prizes_smoke.rb` uses five isolated Chromium browsers and disables fallback polling to prove push delivery. It checks the settings form, last-pick speed ranking, ordered leaderboard and phone places, live Top/Draw/Reset marks, zero-point exclusion, redraw eligibility independent of speed, mobile fit, and persisted results after reload. Locally the five-screen question update took about 140 ms. Model, integration, and channel tests cover timing sums, repeated choices, disconnected players, exact ties, empty/all-zero games, too many prizes, cutoff ties, snapshot settings, protected prize actions, and score privacy.
+
+## Per-question timer
+
+- **Timer minutes** and **Timer seconds** are nonnegative integer quiz settings, both defaulting to 0. Their sum in seconds is snapshotted into a game when it is created. Zero disables timing. Editing settings does not change an existing game. Seconds greater than 59 are accepted and normalized in the displayed M:SS countdown.
+- Each Start/Next persists `question_opened_at` and `question_deadline_at` together under the game lock. Reloading does not modify them. Finishing early retains the original deadline for history but closes the question immediately.
+- `bin/serve` supervises Puma and a local Rails timer worker. The worker checks the indexed deadlines every 100 ms and closes overdue questions using the same state and post-commit notifications as Finish. It catches up after restart without a job queue, external service, or connected browser. If either process exits, the launcher terminates the other; shutdown signals are forwarded to both.
+- Screen requests also reconcile overdue questions. Answer writes check the deadline under the shared game lock and reject picks at or after it. If a late answer discovers expiration, closure commits before the conflict is raised, avoiding rollback of the automatic finish. Stale sweeps recheck the freshly locked record, so they cannot close the next question early.
+- Only the authenticated moderator's snapshot contains the deadline. The countdown computes remaining time from that deadline, rather than decrementing a counter. Server timestamps synchronize its clock on initial load and subsequent responses; countdown ticks do not change the screen revision or replace forms. Players receive neither a countdown nor deadline metadata.
+- Timer settings are deliberately absent from export/import. Imported quizzes start with a disabled timer, even if uploaded JSON contains unknown timer keys. The portable format is unchanged.
+
+Verification: `bin/rails test`, then `bundle exec ruby script/timer_smoke.rb` against `bin/serve` with matching credentials. The Chromium smoke test checks settings, M:SS ticks, reload persistence, player privacy, automatic and early finishing, fresh deadlines on Next, and reconnecting after expiration. No browser driver download is required.
